9. Common interview questions
Q1. Is one word always equal to one token?
No. A word may be represented by one token or multiple tokens, depending on the tokenizer.
Q2. What happens if the context window is exceeded?
The API or model interface may reject the request, truncate some content, or require the application to reduce or reorganize its input. The exact behavior depends on the system.
Q3. Does a larger context window always produce better answers?
No. A larger window allows more information to be supplied, but irrelevant or conflicting information can still reduce answer quality. Retrieval relevance and context organization remain important.
Q4. How do you reduce token usage in a production GenAI application?
Use relevant retrieval, limit unnecessary history, compress or summarize context carefully, avoid redundant instructions, and monitor input and output usage.
Q5. What is the difference between vocabulary size and context window?
Vocabulary size is the number of token units a tokenizer can represent. Context window size is the amount of tokenized context a model can process in one request or processing context.
